     the chat waits on @wj1_intro_done.value before Alma goes on. -->

<Vertical id="wj1_intro">
<Markdown>
Hello! In this activity you will interact with fictional characters in a scenario created to help you
think about your writing process. There will be a chat on the left side of the screen that you can advance
with the 'continue' button. Sometimes activities, prompts, and text will appear here on the right side.
When this happens, please read and interact with it before advancing the chat.
</Markdown>
<Done id="wj1_intro_done" label="Next" />
</Vertical>

<Markdown id="wj1_welcome">
## Writing Journal 2: Who Am I As a Writer?

It's the first week of your First-Year Composition course, and **Professor Ellington** has just assigned
a Writing Process Journal. It reads:

> **Who am I as a writer?**

Here's the thing: maybe you think about yourself as a writer, or maybe you don't. Either way, you
probably write constantly — messages, notes, posts, replies. A lot of the writing we do in our daily
lives doesn't feel like the kind of writing we're asked to do for school and work.

For this journal, you'll start noticing the writing you already do — not only papers and assignments,
but all writing, from texts to lists — and think about what it says about you as a writer.

So when **Alma**, who took ENGL 1010 last year, texts you to come to the **Writers' Circle**, you decide
to go. The Writing Circle is a few students who meet in the library to talk about what they're writing
and what's tripping them up. Lee and Richard (the other two members of the writing circle) are already
mid-conversation.

**By the end of this journal you will have:**

- built a **Writer Board**: a map of the kinds and frequency of writing you actually do, both for school
  and outside of school;
- thought about **why** you do different kinds of writing;
- read through the Circle's eight writer personalities and chosen the one that sounds most like you — or
  written your own combination of them; and
- told a story from your writing life that shows why that personality fits you, or where it doesn't
  quite capture your writing life.

There are no wrong answers here and no grade for being one kind of writer or another. Work through the
conversation on the left until it directs you to content on the right. When you finish something on the
right, scroll back up to the chat box and continue the conversation.
</Markdown>


